# Building, deploying and entering Claude Code

This directory was written by `ansible-playbook aslib.infra.claude_setup`. Run every
command below **from this directory**, on a machine with ansible-core and the
aslib.infra collection installed (see aslib.infra's README). Every run writes its
output to a file of its own in `logs/`, such as `logs/deploy-20261003T081502Z.log`.

| File | What it is | Whose | In git |
|---|---|---|---|
| `inventory.yml` | The servers to deploy to | Yours | Yes |
| `group_vars/all/project.yml` | The project's settings: written with every default, then yours | Yours | Yes |
| `container.env` | The container's environment variables and secrets, e.g. an API key | Yours | **No** |
| `../Containerfile`, `../managed-settings.json` | The image and the policy | Yours | Yes |
| `group_vars/all/defaults.yml` | Every setting at aslib.infra's default; `project.yml` wins | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `image.yml`, `deploy.yml`, `build-and-deploy.yml`, `remove.yml` | Build, deploy, both in one go, remove | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `templates/service.container.j2` | The Quadlet file: how Podman and systemd run the service | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `update-playbooks.yml`, `ansible.cfg`, `.gitignore`, this guide | | aslib.infra | Yes |
| `images/`, `logs/`, `*.bak` | The built image, the log of every run, copies an update keeps | | No |

Your files are never overwritten. aslib.infra's are brought up to date by every
update (step 5), which keeps the old version of each changed file as `<file>.bak`.

## 1. Set up

`ansible-playbook aslib.infra.claude_setup` wrote this project. Before the first
deploy, look at what is yours: the tools in `../Containerfile`, the policy in
`../managed-settings.json` (see `../README.md`), the servers in `inventory.yml`
(this machine by default) and the settings in `group_vars/all/project.yml`.

## 2. Build and deploy

```sh
ansible-playbook build-and-deploy.yml    # add --ask-become-pass if sudo asks for a password
```

This runs `image.yml`, then `deploy.yml`.

**The image** (`image.yml`) needs Podman 3.4 or newer (RHEL 9 or Ubuntu 22.04) and
access to Red Hat's registry and Anthropic's repository (or mirrors, see the
settings). It lands in `images/` as `{{ __claude_code_name }}-<version>.tar`, with the
newest version of the repository's channel unless `claude_code_version` pins one.
A build without a new version or a changed Containerfile leaves everything as it was.

**The deploy** (`deploy.yml`) works as root (through sudo) and, on each server,
runs the image as the always-on service `{{ __claude_code_name }}`, as an account of
its own whose home is `{{ __claude_code_dir }}/`. Claude keeps its login and history
there; `config/managed-settings.json` in it is the policy, read-only for Claude.
With `podman_service_shared`, Claude also works in the shared zone,
`/opt/containers/shared/`, which every service of aslib.infra may read and write.
It installs tmux when it is missing, and the command `{{ __claude_code_name }}` for root.

What a server needs: RHEL 9.2 or newer with Podman, tmux or a repository that has
it (RHEL's BaseOS does), and SSH access with sudo (or as root) for Ansible.

## 3. Enter it

As root on the server:

```sh
sudo -i
{{ __claude_code_name }}                # your own session, claude-<you>; arguments go to claude, e.g. --continue
```

Each root user gets a tmux session of their own, running Claude in the container.
Detach with **Ctrl-b d**: Claude keeps working. Run `{{ __claude_code_name }}` again to
return. The first session asks you to sign in once for everyone: the sign-in page
shows a code to paste into Claude.

A new image restarts the service and ends the running sessions; the conversations
stay, so `{{ __claude_code_name }} --continue` resumes the last one.

## 4. Check on it

```sh
ansible-playbook aslib.infra.podman_overview      # every service, Claude Code included
```

On the server: `sudo journalctl _SYSTEMD_USER_UNIT={{ __claude_code_name }}.service`
and `sudo tmux ls` (the sessions).

## 5. Update

- **Claude Code:** `ansible-playbook build-and-deploy.yml` builds the newest version
  and deploys it when there is one.
- **aslib.infra:** install the newer release, then `ansible-playbook update-playbooks.yml`.
  It brings aslib.infra's files up to date, keeping each changed one as `<file>.bak`.
  New settings appear in `group_vars/all/defaults.yml`; copy one into `project.yml`
  to change it.
- **The policy:** edit `../managed-settings.json`, then `ansible-playbook deploy.yml`.
  The service restarts with it, which ends the running sessions, as a new image
  does (step 3).

## 6. Remove it

```sh
ansible-playbook remove.yml                                     # keeps {{ __claude_code_dir }}, with the login
ansible-playbook remove.yml -e podman_service_remove_data=true  # removes it as well
```

## Download sources

`image.yml` downloads; `deploy.yml` does not, but installs tmux and acl (for the
shared zone) from the server's repositories when they are missing.

| What | Set in the project | Set on the machine that builds |
|---|---|---|
| Red Hat's UBI 9 image, from `registry.access.redhat.com` | `claude_code_base_image` | `registries.conf` |
| Claude Code, from `downloads.claude.ai` | `claude_code_repository`, `claude_code_key_url` | |
| The tools in the Containerfile | | Red Hat's UBI repositories, as set up in the base image |

Claude itself needs `api.anthropic.com` and, to sign in, `claude.ai`, `claude.com`
and `platform.claude.com`.
